===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|usually|transitive}} To [[cover#Verb|cover]] with or [[encase#Verb|encase]] in concrete (building material).
#: {{ux|en|I hate grass, so I '''concreted''' over my lawn.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Francis Lynde|title=David Vallory|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Charles Scribner's Sons|year=1919|page=322|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/davidvallory00lyndgoog/page/322/mode/1up|passage=In odd moments David had made an estimate on the cost of shooting down the menace in the eastern tunnel drifting and '''concreting''' the gash which would be left by the blasting out of the fissure material.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Reshad Feild|title=Here to Heal|location=Shaftesbury, Dorset|publisher=w:Element Books|year=1985|page=84|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/heretoheal00feil/page/84/mode/1up|isbn=0-906-54080-1|passage=At first they could not remember anything out of the ordinary, and then the farmer's wife remarked that they had changed the pattern of the milking parlour by '''concreting''' the area where the cows were waiting.}}
#* {{RQ:LATimes|author=Louis Sahagun|title=A journey of discovery on the L.A. River|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230708174854/https://www.latimes.com/local/la-xpm-2010-jul-30-la-me-river-20100729-story.html|date=1 August 2010|passage=Frequent catastrophic floods prompted civic leaders in the 1930s to transform the river into a flood-control channel to protect the burgeoning flatlands. Nearly the entire 51-mile river bottom was '''concreted''' over, except a few spots where the water table was too high.}}
# {{lb|en|usually|transitive}} To [[solidify#Verb|solidify]]: to change from being [[abstract]] to being concrete (actual, real).
#* {{RQ:Twain Pudd'nhead Wilson|pages=55–56|pageref=55|passage={{...}} the necessity of recognizing this relation outwardly and of perfecting herself in the forms required to express the recognition, had moved her to such diligence and faithfulness in practicing these forms that this exercise soon '''concreted''' itself into habit; it became automatic and unconscious; then a natural result followed: {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=C. Reinold Noyes|title=The Institution of Property|location=New York, N.Y.; Toronto, Ont.; London|publisher=w:Longmans, Green and Co.; w:Humphrey Milford|year=1936|page=536|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/institutionofpro0000unse/page/536/mode/1up|passage=Just so economics has '''concreted''' the concept of capital. The law needs a term for the material and quasi-material objects of property.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Kurt Riezler|title=Physics and Reality:{{nb...|Lectures of Aristotle on Modern Physics at an International Congress of Science}}|location=New Haven, C.T.|publisher=w:Yale University Press|year=1940|page=104|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/physicsreality0000kurt/page/104/mode/1up|passage=It is only such a [[logos#Noun|logos]] that can '''concrete''' the concrete and make reality real.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|archaic}} To [[unite#Verb|unite]] or [[coalesce#Verb|coalesce]] into a solid mass.
#* {{RQ:Newton Opticks|edition=4th|book=3|page=363|passage=When any ſaline Liquor is evaporated to a Cuticle and let cool, the Salt '''concretes''' in regular Figures; which argues, that the Particles of the Salt before they '''concreted''', floated in the Liquor at equal diſtances in rank and file, and by conſequence that they acted upon one another by ſome Power which at equal diſtances is equal, at unequal diſtances unequal.}}
#* {{RQ:Arbuthnot Aliments|page=46|passage=The Blood of ſome Perſons who have dy'd of the Plague could not be made to '''concrete''', by reaſon of the Putrefaction already begun.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:Everard Home|title=On the Formation of Fat in the Intestines of living Animals|journal=[[w:Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society|Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London]]|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed by}} [[w:William Bulmer (printer)|W&#91;illiam&#93; Bulmer]] and Co.{{nb...|Cleveland-Row, St. James's}}|year=1813|page=152|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/s3id13528510/page/152/mode/1up|issn=0261-0523|oclc=1697286|passage=At three years old, her mother observed something come from her, as she walked across the room, which, when examined, was found to be fat in a liquid state, which '''concreted''' when cold.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Jonathan Pereira|chapter=''Pista'cia Lentis'cus'', Linn. L. E. D.—''The Mastic'' or ''Lentisk Tree''.|title=The Elements of Materia Medica;{{nb...|Comprehending the Natural History, Preparation, Properties, Composition, Effects and Uses of Medicines}}|location=London|publisher=Longman, Orme, Brown, Green, and Longmans|year=1840|volume_plain=Part II|page=1183|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/b3309598x_0002/page/1183/mode/1up|passage=The mastic which '''concretes''' on the stem is called ''mastic in the tear'', while that which falls to the earth constitutes ''common mastic''.}}

====Usage notes====
* Etymologically, the antonym of ''concrete'' is {{m|en|secrete||exude, yield}}, but the meanings of the two verbs have diverged so widely that this is scarcely noticed today.

